# The build context is the repository root, because the server is one member of
# a uv workspace and the build needs the lock file and the shared packages. That
# makes this file load-bearing rather than tidy: without it the context is the
# 130 MB working tree, of which 116 MB is `.venv`, and every build ships all of
# it to the daemon before reading a single instruction. Over a network to a Pi
# that is the whole build time.
#
# It is also a correctness problem, not only a speed one. `COPY server/ server/`
# would copy a host `.venv` if one existed there, and a virtualenv built against
# the host's interpreter landing in the image is a broken image that takes a
# while to diagnose.

# Virtualenvs. The image builds its own; a host one is wrong for it by
# construction -- wrong interpreter, wrong platform, absolute shebangs.
.venv/
venv/
**/.venv/

# The same argument as `.venv`, one order of magnitude larger: `web/node_modules`
# is 339 MB of the working tree, and the Node stage installs its own from the
# lockfile. Without this line every build sends all of it to the daemon and then
# `COPY web/ ./` drops it *on top of* the tree `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`
# just made -- so a `buildx --platform linux/arm64` for the Pi gets a rollup and
# an esbuild built for x86, and a developer who has never run `pnpm install`
# gets a different image from one who has.
**/node_modules/

# The previous local `pnpm build`. The Node stage makes its own and would
# overwrite this, so copying it in buys a slower context and a chance of
# shipping a stale build if the stage ever stops rebuilding it.
web/dist/

# History and tooling state. Nothing in the build reads them.
.git/
.github/
.gitignore
.superpowers/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
**/__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# Bytecode and databases from a local run. An `ors.db` copied into the image
# would be shadowed by the volume at runtime, so it would not even be used --
# just published, with whatever is in it.
*.db
*.sqlite3
.env

# Planning prose and the deploy files themselves. The Dockerfile is read by the
# builder before the context is consulted, so excluding this directory does not
# exclude the Dockerfile.
docs/
deploy/

# The daemon. It is a systemd unit on the rack and never runs in this image; its
# `pyproject.toml` is the one thing the build needs, to resolve the workspace,
# and that is re-admitted below.
daemon/
!daemon/pyproject.toml
